;; The single version source. //src:clojars reads it for the pom, the release
;; workflow compares it against the tag, and //bazel/tools:version_consistency
;; keeps the README install snippet in agreement on every bazel test //...
;;
;; 0.1.10, because 0.1.7, 0.1.8 and 0.1.9 are all spent. None exists on Clojars and
;; none can be redeployed ("Non-SNAPSHOT redeploy"), which is why the numbering
;; jumps from 0.1.6:
;;
;;   0.1.7  the publisher uploaded a .sha256; Clojars answers that with 400, after
;;          it had already accepted the jar. Fixed in rules_clj 0.2.1.
;;   0.1.8  every file was accepted with a 201 and the version still never appeared:
;;          Clojars treats the maven-metadata.xml upload as the signal that a deploy
;;          is complete, and the publisher was not sending one. Fixed in rules_clj
;;          0.2.2.
;;   0.1.9  the artifacts uploaded, then the publish refused to finish: reading the
;;          version list got a 302 to Clojars' read mirror and the client would not
;;          follow a redirect. Fixed in rules_clj 0.2.3, which also made --dry-run
;;          perform the reads a real publish performs.
;;
;; Both were found by releasing, and neither could have been found by a dry run.
;;
;; 0.1.5 and not 0.1.0: protoc-gen-clojure's emitter already states runtime
;; floors in generated-code comments — 0.1.3 for the 3-arg rt/message class
;; hint, 0.1.5 for dotted nested-type lookup. The first published artifact
;; satisfies both, so those comments point at a version that really exists.
;;
;; 0.2.0: the non-generated prototype is no longer a DynamicMessage but the
;; compiled codec's own Message implementation. Same bytes, same reflective
;; API, but a different concrete class — anything downstream that tested for
;; DynamicMessage notices, hence the minor bump.
;;
;; 0.2.1: rt/prototype. 0.2.0 left a library that manufactures prototypes from
;; a bare Descriptor — clj-grpc's marshallers — on DynamicMessage for inbound
;; parsing unless it re-derived the emitter's class-hint rule itself. Now one
;; call resolves the arm the generated namespace got.
;;
;; 0.2.2: protobuf-java 4.36.1. No change of behaviour; the runtime line moves.
;;
;; 0.2.3: two correctness fixes. A field whose Java accessor would shadow a
;; method every message has (serialized_size -> getSerializedSize) read the
;; message's own size on the hinted arm; and aliased enum values read back as
;; different names on different arms.
;; 0.2.4: building a compiled message no longer copies its slot array. The
;; message takes the array and the builder copies on its next write, which is
;; 40 B less per message built — a quarter of the build-half allocation on a
;; list of small messages. No behavioural change: what a built message holds
;; is still untouchable through the builder that made it, and
;; //test:message_test drives every mutator to pin it.
;; 0.2.5: no process-wide monitor on the per-message path. The required-field
;; memo (reached from .build on every message, nested ones included) and the
;; parser registry (reached on every decode) were Collections.synchronizedMaps,
;; which lock on reads too, so a cache hit serialized every thread in the
;; process against every other. Both are per-type facts and now live on the
;; CompiledType as delays. Compiled-arm encode went from 0.99x to 8.48x across
;; 1->8 threads, 10.5x the throughput at eight, and 23% faster on one thread.
;; Anyone holding a compiled-vs-interop comparison from before this wants to
;; re-run it. //bench:contention is the probe.
;;
;; Found after the release, and fixed by it: the shared memo was also wrong on
;; cyclic proto2 graphs. It cached a cycle-broken false as a nested type's own
;; answer, so that type reported no required field anywhere in its closure,
;; the initialization check skipped on that basis, and a message carrying an
;; uninitialized sub-message built without complaint instead of throwing
;; UninitializedMessageException. Per-type memos cannot do this, because the
;; cut stays local to the walk that made it. Pinned by
;; //test:message_test's required-fields-through-a-cycle.
;; 0.3.0: the typed read path's surface — rt/compiled-message?, rt/slot,
;; rt/slot-of — so generated code can read a compiled message's slots directly
;; instead of calling codec/get-field per field. Additive: the six contract
;; symbols are untouched and proto->X still returns a record, so nothing
;; existing breaks and no consumer must change anything.
;;
;; A minor rather than a patch because of what it COMMITS, not what it
;; changes. Slot representation is public contract from here: generated files
;; bake a per-field conversion chosen from it, so altering what a slot holds
;; is source-breaking for every generated file in existence, exactly like
;; altering a contract symbol's signature. It constrains slot contents and not
;; the layout around them — 0.2.4 and 0.2.5 both changed that layout freely.
;; //test:message_test's the-typed-read-path-surface keeps the promise.
;;
;; Also here, neither consumer-visible: the benchmark gained the two
;; production-shaped tiers it never had (1 KB at 30 leaves and at 120), and
;; //bench:option_c measures the ceiling of the whole typed-read family.
;; 0.3.1: documentation only — the published source is otherwise identical to
;; 0.3.0, and nothing needs this release. It exists because rt/slot-of's
;; docstring now states the guarantee generated code actually relies on: the
;; index is the field's DECLARATION index in its descriptor, (.getIndex fd),
;; never an internal ordering, so a generator holding the descriptor may bake
;; the literal and skip the call. protoc-gen-clojure 0.7.0 does exactly that,
;; and it works against 0.3.0 — this only puts the promise in the artifact a
;; consumer reads rather than leaving it in this repo's tests and docs.
{:version "0.3.1"}
